merge queue: a failed review is restartable — the person's verb, and the queue's own bounded retry

The operator's report, verbatim: *"so merge queue has 4 failed items, we need a
way to restart them"*.

Four gatekeeper reviews had sat for hours on `glm-coding/glm-5.3`, whose
provider answers `http 429: Weekly/Monthly Limit Exhausted. Your limit will
reset at 2026-10-12 15:01:48`; the daemon that spawned them predated this tree's
re-point of `~/.config/letibot/leticode.toml` and predated the work that makes a
429 take the round again elsewhere. A quota is weather, and weather is not the
defect. The defect is that **a review whose attempt failed had no way to be
attempted again**, so four entries were stuck behind a transient condition with
nothing in the queue able to say *try that one again now*.

**A failed attempt was recorded where a judgement goes, and that is what made it
terminal.** `Harness::review_outcome` mapped a gatekeeper child whose turn died
(a `429`, a spawn that could not happen), or which answered with no readable
`verdict:` block, to `decision: "failed"` / `"unreadable"` / `"could_not_start"`
— written into `merge_review.decision` with `answered_ms` set.
`mergequeue::review_gate` reads any word outside the gatekeeper's closed set as
a **Refusal**, so a failure parked the entry `Failed` on the evidence *"the
reviewer's verdict on `x` is `failed`, which is not one of accept, reject,
needs_human"*: a judgement nobody made, on a row a person reads. And because
`answered_ms` was set, `Harness::serve_reviews` — which filters on
`answered_ms.is_none()` — never started another one. So the four are not *may
land* and not *may not*; they were entries behind weather with a row that said
somebody had judged them.

**What the store actually held for the four**, checked before this was written:
four rows in `merge_queue` (three `failed`, one `conflict`) and four in
`merge_review`, and all four reviews had `answered_ms` set with real closed-set
words — `needs_human`, `reject`, `accept`, `needs_human`. So for *these* four no
failure had been recorded as a verdict; their four children
(`…-1791569790219{697204,823991,878554,927325}`, 70/129/108/252 rows) had
walked the 429 ladder and then answered. The path above is live in the code all
the same, and it is the one this closes.

**The attempt is its own fact (schema v20).** `merge_review` gains `attempts`,
`failed_ms` and `failure`; `ReviewRecord` carries the same three. `decision`
stays NULL for a failed attempt, and `answered_ms` stays NULL with it — which is
what lets the queue ask again at all. A verdict clears the failure; a restart
clears all three.

**One policy, read by both halves.** `mergequeue::review_retry(review, now_ms)`
answers `Due | Wait { in_ms } | Exhausted` from the row and the clock, and
`MAX_REVIEW_ATTEMPTS = 3` with a wait that doubles from `REVIEW_RETRY_BASE_MS =
30_000` is `http_retry_after`'s SHAPE — a small number, a wait that grows, and
then a report — rather than a second policy invented here. Its numbers are its
own and say why: one beat of the HTTP ladder is a retry inside a turn, and one
beat of this is a whole reviewer, a child session and a turn of its own. The
queue's pass rings under it, and **`Harness::serve_reviews` spawns under it** —
that second reader is load-bearing, because a failed child's own settlement
wakes its parent, so without it the host would re-spawn once per wake and the
bound would be a bound on nothing. Three attempts and then the entry parks
`Failed` with the failure on its row; no loop spends a model turn per beat.

**And a failed entry says so where a person looks.** The failure is put on the
entry's row FIRST, because the pane hard-truncates that line: `http 429:
Weekly/Monthly Limit Exhausted. Your limit will reset at …` opens it, and the
queue's own words follow. Two things in the head had to move for that to be
readable at all, and neither is in rano: the review half said *the reviewer has
been asked and has not answered* over an attempt that had already died, and now
says `no verdict` (the wire's `MergeReview` gains `failure`, defaulted, so no
`PROTOCOL_VERSION` bump — it stays 37); and the row draws
`registry::short_id` rather than a forty-character `s-…-sub-…`, which had been
eating the whole line and truncating the reason off the end. `Enter` on the row
still wraps the evidence whole.

**The person's act, in two spellings and one function.** `/queue restart
ENTRY-ID` and `r` on a parked row in the queue pane both send `Action::Slash {
line: "queue restart …" }`, so the key and the typed verb cannot drift — and it
rides the existing `ClientFrame::Slash`, which is the tree's own door for *a
daemon act with feedback on the session log*. No new frame, no protocol bump.
`Store::restart_review` is one transaction: the entry back to `waiting` and its
review row back to *nobody has answered*, with the entry's `UPDATE` guarded
`WHERE state IN ('failed','conflict','stale')` so the ROW arbitrates and a
second press moves nothing.

**A restart re-attempts the same entry and never a second reviewer beside a live
one.** `mergequeue::restartable` refuses an attempt in flight (`answered_ms`
NULL and `failed_ms` NULL: asked for, neither answered nor failed) and refuses
an entry that is not parked, each by name. That is why the operator pressing it
twice is safe: the first ask leaves the row outstanding, so the second is the
in-flight case and says so. It announces the move as the queue's own
`MergeEntryMoved` — a restart IS an entry move — published after the rows are
written, `move_to`'s own order.

**The model a restarted review gets is the model a fresh review gets.** A restart
re-spawns through the ordinary subagent door, so it resolves `[roles.gatekeeper]
model` → `leticode.toml`'s `subagent_model` → the parent's model as the file
stands NOW. That is exactly what makes it a restart here rather than a second
attempt at the same wall: the four were on `glm-coding/glm-5.3` and the file has
since been re-pointed at deepseek. Nothing about the restart names a model, and
nothing should — a model pinned into a restart would be a restart onto the model
that just failed.

Tests: `mergequeue::restart::{a_restart_re_attempts_exactly_one_review,
a_second_restart_does_not_double_spawn,
a_live_review_cannot_be_restarted_into_a_duplicate,
a_review_that_cannot_be_asked_parks_with_the_failure_on_the_row,
the_review_retry_is_bounded_and_doubles, the_failure_leads_the_row_it_is_written_on}`;
`harness::gatekeeper_reviews::a_failed_attempt_is_not_recorded_as_a_verdict` and
`a_readable_answer_still_writes_the_verdict`;
`store::tests::{a_failed_attempt_is_its_own_row_and_a_restart_clears_it,
a_v19_store_gains_the_attempt_columns}`; `slash::the_queue_sub_verb_is_the_restart_and_nothing_else`;
and the pane's own `a_failed_review_says_so_on_the_panes_row`,
`the_row_draws_the_short_id_so_the_reason_fits` and
`the_restart_key_sends_the_verb_for_the_row_under_the_cursor`.
